faq:
  - q: "What is cured in place pipe liner testing?"
    a: "A sample of the actual cured liner is cut at the end of the installation, usually from the tail that extends into the manhole or access pit, and sent to a laboratory. The lab measures its wall thickness and bends specimens to failure to determine flexural strength and flexural modulus. The results are compared against the minimums in the specification and the design thickness the liner was engineered to."
  - q: "What are the minimum values for a CIPP liner?"
    a: "The most common specification minimums, drawn from ASTM F1216 and written into city and state standards, are a flexural strength of 4,500 psi and a flexural modulus of elasticity of 250,000 psi when tested per ASTM D790. Where tensile strength is specified, 3,000 psi per ASTM D638 is typical. Enhanced resins and glass liners test well above these."
  - q: "How is the sample taken?"
    a: "For a representative result, the sample has to cure under the same conditions as the liner in the pipe. Specifications call for the liner tail to be inverted into a short section of pipe of the same diameter held in a heat sink such as sandbags, so the sample is restrained and loses heat the way the buried liner does. It is cut after cool-down."
  - q: "Why does the design use half the tested modulus?"
    a: "Plastics creep: under a steady load their stiffness falls over time. CIPP design uses a long-term flexural modulus, conventionally 50 percent of the short-term tested value over a 50-year life, unless the manufacturer has ASTM D2990 long-term test data that supports a different retention factor. The 4,500 and 250,000 psi short-term minimums therefore translate to 2,250 and 125,000 psi for design."
  - q: "Is liner testing done on residential jobs?"
    a: "Rarely as a lab test; the cost is out of proportion to a one-day lateral job. What a homeowner should get instead is the cure log showing time and temperature, the manufacturer's published properties for the liner system, and the final camera recording. If you want a sample tested, ask before the job so the crew can prepare a restrained sample."
  - q: "What happens if the liner fails the test?"
    a: "On public contracts, a failing sample triggers a review: the liner may be retested from another sample, accepted at a reduced price, or in a clear structural failure removed and replaced. The thickness check is the most common failure point, which is why specifications allow a minimum of 87.5 percent of the design thickness at any single point."
