<p class="cl-lead">A cured in place pipe liner is manufactured in the ground, on the day, by the crew. That is the method's strength and its risk: unlike a pipe from a factory, nobody has tested this exact piece before it went in. Liner testing is how owners close that gap. Here is what gets tested, what the numbers mean and how much of it applies to a house sewer versus a city main.</p>

<h2>Why a liner gets tested at all</h2>
<p>The <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe">cured in place pipe</a> design method assumes the old pipe will eventually contribute nothing and the liner must carry soil load, groundwater pressure and traffic on its own, for decades. The design equations in ASTM F1216 turn those loads into a required wall thickness for a given resin stiffness. If the resin did not cure fully, if the wet-out left dry spots, or if the liner stretched thin on installation, the real pipe is weaker than the design. Testing catches that by measuring the real thing.</p>

<h2>How the sample is taken</h2>
<p>The sample has to represent the buried liner, so it must cure the same way. Specifications require the installer to extend the liner tail into a short length of pipe of the same diameter at the manhole or access pit, packed in a heat sink, typically sandbags, so it is restrained and sheds heat like the liner in the ground. After the controlled cool-down, the tail is cut off and the restrained piece becomes the sample. A sample cut from liner that cured loose in open air will test too well; an unrestrained sample is not a valid sample. Each sample has to yield at least three test specimens, and five are preferred for the bending test.</p>

<h2>The tests</h2>
<table class="cl-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Standard</th><th>What it measures</th><th>Typical minimum</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Wall thickness</td><td>ASTM D2122 (or D5813 method)</td><td>Eight measurements around the ring, averaged, after removing any inner coating and outer membrane.</td><td>Average at or above design thickness; no single point below 87.5% of it.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Flexural strength</td><td>ASTM D790</td><td>Stress at which a bent specimen breaks.</td><td>4,500 psi</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Flexural modulus</td><td>ASTM D790</td><td>Stiffness in bending, the number the thickness design depends on.</td><td>250,000 psi</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tensile strength</td><td>ASTM D638</td><td>Pull-apart strength; specified for pressure pipe and some sewer specs.</td><td>3,000 psi</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Chemical resistance</td><td>ASTM D543 / F1216 appendix</td><td>Specimens soaked in sewage-like solutions; properties must not fall below the minimums.</td><td>No loss below the values above</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Long-term creep</td><td>ASTM D2990</td><td>Stiffness retained under sustained load; used to justify a retention factor other than 50%.</td><td>Manufacturer data</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Reports must state the conditioning of the specimens (temperature and humidity) and are rejected without it. Testing is done by the contractor or an independent laboratory approved by the owner's engineer; many owners require the independent lab.</p>

<h2>Reading the numbers</h2>
<p>Two values do most of the work. <strong>Flexural modulus</strong> is stiffness. The design used a number for it, usually the manufacturer's published short-term value, and if the tested liner comes in lower, the liner is less stiff than designed and may be undersized for the load. <strong>Thickness</strong> is the other lever: a liner that is stiff enough but thin fails the same way. Strength matters less than people expect, because a buried liner is governed by buckling under external pressure, which is a stiffness-and-thickness problem.</p>
<p>The design does not use the tested modulus directly. Thermoset plastics creep, so a <strong>long-term modulus</strong> of 50 percent of the short-term value is assumed over a 50-year life unless D2990 data says otherwise. That is why a specification can list 250,000 psi as a test minimum and 125,000 psi as the design value in the same paragraph. Other design inputs you will see: soil unit weight around 120 pcf, groundwater taken to the surface, AASHTO HS-20 traffic load, 2 percent ovality minimum, a safety factor of 2.0 and a 5 percent resin-migration allowance on thickness. The site's <a href="/p/cipp-specifications">CIPP specifications page</a> collects the material side of this.</p>

<h2>What applies to a house sewer</h2>
<p>Almost no residential lateral gets a lab test; a few hundred dollars of testing on a one-day job is out of proportion, and specifications for private work rarely call for it. What you can and should ask for instead:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The <strong>liner and resin system by name</strong>, and the manufacturer's data sheet showing its tested flexural properties. Those numbers should clear the minimums above with room to spare.</li>
  <li>The <strong>design thickness</strong> for your pipe, and how the installer arrived at it.</li>
  <li>The <strong>cure log</strong>: time and temperature from the boiler, steam or UV controller. This is the residential equivalent of a test report, and the <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/installation">cured in place pipe installation</a> page explains where in the day it is produced.</li>
  <li>The <strong>final camera recording</strong>, which shows cure quality visually: a hard, smooth, wrinkle-free surface.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you want a physical sample tested anyway, say so when you book. The crew can set up a restrained sample at the cleanout, and any materials lab that runs D790 can test it.</p>

<h2>Why this matters when choosing a contractor</h2>
<p>A contractor who works public jobs lives with sampling and lab reports and is used to the discipline it imposes: full cure, careful cool-down, verified thickness. That habit carries over to private work. Asking a residential installer whether they have had liners lab-tested, and what the results were, is a fair and revealing question. The <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/training-and-certifications">training and certifications</a> page lists the other credentials to ask about, and the <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/equipment">cured in place pipe equipment</a> page covers the data loggers and cure controls that generate the records in the first place.</p>
